Subject: [PATCH] audio: AudioContext runs at 48 kHz for every profile

A page reads AudioContext.sampleRate, and baseLatency is the buffer size
divided by it. On a Linux host without a sound card the host rate is
44100 Hz, which gives a Windows profile a baseLatency of 0.010884 where
real Windows reads 0.01. When the profile names a platform, the renderer
uses 48 kHz, and the audio service resamples if the real device runs
another rate. Host mode and OfflineAudioContext are unchanged.

--- a/content/renderer/media/renderer_webaudiodevice_impl.cc
+++ b/content/renderer/media/renderer_webaudiodevice_impl.cc
@@ -279,6 +279,20 @@
           *profile->audio_hardware_buffer_frames());
       original_sink_params_.set_hardware_capabilities(std::nullopt);
     }
+
+    // The output rate is the claimed machine's as well. The buffer above is a
+    // frame count at the claimed device's rate, and that rate is 48 kHz: every
+    // Windows and macOS reference measured it, and 48 kHz is the clock
+    // PipeWire, the sound server of current Linux desktops, runs by default.
+    // Left to the host, a machine without a sound card answers 44100 here
+    // (AudioParameters::UnavailableDeviceParams), which turns Windows' 480
+    // frames into a baseLatency of 0.010884 that no Windows machine reports.
+    // The stream still plays on any host, because the audio service resamples
+    // a rate its device does not run, as it already does for a context created
+    // with its own sampleRate.
+    if (profile->ua_platform().has_value()) {
+      original_sink_params_.set_sample_rate(48000);
+    }
   }
 
   SendLogMessage(base::StringPrintf(
